%N A379427 Numbers m such that prime(k)*m + prime(k+1), for k=1..8 all are primes.
%C A379427 There are no values of m such that prime(k)*m + prime(k+1), k=1..9 are all prime. See A108117 for a proof.
%C A379427 From _Miquel Cerda_, Oct 19 2026: (Start)
%C A379427 All terms satisfy m == 14 (mod 30). Indeed, m must be even, since otherwise 3*m+5 is even and greater than 2; m == 2 (mod 3), since the other two residue classes make 2*m+3 or 5*m+7 divisible by 3; and m == 4 (mod 5), since m == 0, 1, 2, or 3 (mod 5) makes 3*m+5, 2*m+3, 7*m+11, or 17*m+19 divisible by 5, respectively.
%C A379427 Conjecturally, by the Bateman-Horn conjecture, the number N(x) of terms <= x satisfies N(x) ~ C * Integral_{t=2..x} dt / Product_{k=1..8} log(prime(k)*t + prime(k+1)), where C = Product_{q prime} (1-nu(q)/q)/(1-1/q)^8 = 1440.50064158822178..., and nu(q) is the number of residues r modulo q for which q divides Product_{k=1..8} (prime(k)*r + prime(k+1)). One has nu(q) = 1, 2, 4, 4, 6, 5, 7, 7 for q = 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, respectively, and nu(q) = 8 for all primes q >= 23. The value of C has been certified using interval arithmetic. There are 18100 terms <= 10^13, compared with approximately 18057.0 predicted by the conjectural formula.
%C A379427 (End)
%H A379427 Miquel Cerda, <a href="/A379427/b379427.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A379427 Miquel Cerda, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23878424">Admissibility and exact classification of finite sieves for eight linear forms associated with consecutive primes</a> (2026). In Spanish; includes a paper, certified singular constant, verification programs, and supplementary data.
%e A379427 5600384 is OK because 2*5600384+3, 3*5600384+5, 5*5600384+7, 7*5600384+11, 11*5600384+13, 13*5600384+17, 17*5600384+19 and 19*5600384+23 all are primes.
%o A379427 (PARI) \\ See isok from A108117
%o A379427 for(n=1,2*10^9,if(isok(n,8),print1(n", ")))
